WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.729 --> 00:00:04.850
Episode 3 of The Falcon and the Winter Soldier,
"Power Broker," was a fight-filled good time,

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:08.730
packed with plot and globetrotting galore.
It also had its share of Easter eggs. Here

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:10.530
are a few you may have missed.

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:15.150
In "Power Broker," we finally get to see Helmut
Zemo, whose return has been looming since

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:18.640
the series began. Knowing that the villain
is the only way to help track down the origin

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:22.599
of the new Super-Soldier serum, Bucky helps
him break out of a high-security prison with

00:00:22.599 --> 00:00:27.089
ease. After getting the kinda-sorta approval
from Sam, the three men are treated to a private

00:00:27.089 --> 00:00:28.869
plane ride across the globe.

00:00:28.869 --> 00:00:33.500
It turns out that Zemo is rich — really
rich — and there's a reason why. Finally,

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:37.940
we hear this classic Marvel villain note that
he is a baron, a title that's long been associated

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:42.850
with Zemo in the comics, but never yet uttered
in the MCU, until now. Zemo was a member of

00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:48.040
the Sokovian aristocracy, but that was all
taken from him when Ultron decimated the country.

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:52.860
In the comics, the Zemos are actually of German
descent. There have been 13 barons in total,

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:59.100
with Helmut being the most recent. In 1973's
Captain America #168, we learn that Helmut

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:04.280
and his father were both disfigured by Adhesive
X, the most powerful adhesive ever invented,

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:08.830
and thus later donned masks to hide their
faces. There's no instance of Adhesive X or

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:13.500
any kind of disfigurement of Zemo in the MCU,
so his mask-wearing is likely just an homage

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:16.800
to the character and his father.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:20.890
Most Marvel Comics fans are well aware of
Madripoor, a fictional Southeast Asian island.

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:25.340
While a lot of exciting stories have gone
down here, Madripoor is most closely associated

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:29.770
with something we've yet to see in the MCU:
mutants. Namely, Wolverine.

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:35.140
Madripoor is first mentioned in 1985's New
Mutants #32. Wolverine spends a decent amount

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:39.110
of time here, going by the name of Patch.
Even beyond Wolverine, though, the X-Men's

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:43.450
connections to Madripoor are endless. We learn
that Mystique wanted to set up an X-Men haven

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:49.340
there in 2015's All-New X-Men #37. Magneto
uses the island as a temporary holding zone

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:55.630
for time-displaced mutants in 2017's X-Men
Blue #1. With Madripoor now canon in the MCU,

00:01:55.630 --> 00:01:59.550
we can expect several visits to the island
moving forward — possibly led by mutant

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:00.550
characters.

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:04.870
"Power Broker" also contains subtle nods to
Madripoor's Lowtown, including brief shots

00:02:04.870 --> 00:02:09.890
of local businesses made famous in the comics.
The Princess Bar and the Brass Monkey (also

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:15.159
known as the Bronze Monkey), where Sam, Zemo,
and Bucky meet Selby, are iconic Lowtown hotspots.

00:02:15.159 --> 00:02:19.090
In the comics, the Princess Bar is owned by
a man named O'Donnell, and offers entertainment

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:23.380
to residents of both Hightown and Lowtown.
But O'Donnell doesn't run the show on his

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:27.640
own: He has help from his silent partner,
Wolverine. Given Wolverine's stature in Marvel

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:30.870
canon, this is an homage that shouldn't be
taken lightly.

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:35.499
The Falcon and the Winter Soldier has done
a fabulous job so far referencing fan-favorite

00:02:35.499 --> 00:02:40.030
scenes from prior MCU projects. In "Power
Broker," we get a playful throwback to a scene

00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:43.760
from Captain America: Civil War, in which
Sam refuses to move his seat up for Bucky

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:46.459
while they wait for Steve to talk to Sharon
Carter.

00:02:46.459 --> 00:02:49.319
"Can you move your seat up?"

00:02:49.319 --> 00:02:50.319
"No."

00:02:50.319 --> 00:02:53.860
After Sam and Bucky meet Doctor Nagel in the
shipping containers and all hell breaks loose,

00:02:53.860 --> 00:02:58.380
they jump into a swanky convertible which
Zemo has been hiding. Sam takes the back seat,

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:02.480
which offers little leg room, and a callback
to the beloved moment follows.

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:07.329
“You're not gonna move your seat up, are
you?"

00:03:07.329 --> 00:03:08.329
"No."

00:03:08.329 --> 00:03:11.780
Just before diving into Lowtown, Sam is given
a very brief history of the man he is supposed

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:17.459
to be impersonating: Conrad Mack, AKA the
Smiling Tiger. Luckily, Sam resembles Conrad,

00:03:17.459 --> 00:03:22.749
whom he notes has a "bad nickname." First
appearing in 1992's New Warriors #19, Conrad

00:03:22.749 --> 00:03:26.249
is the son of a Vietnam veteran and a cult
member who deals in magic.

00:03:26.249 --> 00:03:30.349
Conrad is also affiliated with the Thunderbolts,
a team of reformed criminals led by Helmut

00:03:30.349 --> 00:03:35.079
Zemo himself. Smiling Tiger doesn't have an
extensive history in the comics, and we likely

00:03:35.079 --> 00:03:38.340
won't see much more of him moving forward
in the MCU.

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:41.999
But his mention does open the door for the
Thunderbolts, also occasionally known as the

00:03:41.999 --> 00:03:46.239
Masters of Evil, who have a long and fascinating
history. The group has further ties to the

00:03:46.239 --> 00:03:50.019
Winter Soldier and Black Widow, who have both
led the team during different moments in its

00:03:50.019 --> 00:03:51.719
history.

00:03:51.719 --> 00:03:55.680
In "Power Broker's" final moments, we get
a shocking cameo from Ayo, a member of the

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:00.249
Wakandan Dora Milaje. We first meet Ayo in
Captain America: Civil War, when she tells

00:04:00.249 --> 00:04:05.359
Natasha Romanoff to move so T'Challa can enter
his car. If Natasha refuses to comply, Ayo

00:04:05.359 --> 00:04:09.569
makes it clear that she "will be moved" by
force. She reappears in Black Panther and

00:04:09.569 --> 00:04:12.069
Avengers: Infinity War, serving under General
Okoye.

00:04:12.069 --> 00:04:16.840
Ayo is here for one purpose, and one purpose
only: To collect Zemo, the man responsible

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:21.250
for the death of King T'Chaka. Many unanswered
questions linger: Namely, how the meeting

00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:25.210
between Bucky and Ayo came to be, and how
the latter knows Zemo is out of prison and

00:04:25.210 --> 00:04:29.380
ready for the taking. Nevertheless, Ayo's
appearance officially ties Wakanda into the

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:31.940
events of The Falcon and the Winter Soldier.

